<file path=".github/copilot-instructions.md">
  1: # GitHub Copilot Instructions
  2: 
  3: ## Project Overview
  4: 
  5: **Project Name**: ScholarFlow  
  6: **Type**: AI-Powered Research Paper Collaboration Hub  
  7: **Architecture**: Monorepo with Next.js frontend and Express.js backend  
  8: **Phase**: Phase 1 Development (MVP) - Authentication Complete ✅  
  9: **Project Start**: August 2025  
 10: **Developer**: Md. Atikur Rahaman (GitHub: Atik203)
 11: 
 12: ## Core Standards
 13: 
 14: ### Package Manager & Environment
 15: 
 16: - Use Yarn Berry (v4) only. Never use `npm` in this repo.
 17: - Dev entrypoint: `yarn dev:turbo` (runs frontend and backend together).
 18: - Keep `.env` in sync. When adding a variable, document it and update both apps' `.env.example`.
 19: - Default ports: frontend `3000`, backend `5000`.
 20: 
 21: ### Technology Stack
 22: 
 23: - Frontend: Next.js 15 + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS + ShadCN UI + Redux Toolkit Query + NextAuth.js + React Hook Form + Zod
 24: - Backend: Express.js + TypeScript + PostgreSQL + Prisma ORM + JWT + bcrypt + Zod
 25: - Database: PostgreSQL with `pgvector` extension (AI features ready)
 26: 
 27: ## ✅ Major Recent Completions
 28: 
 29: - Paper Management System: S3 integration, PDF processing, metadata extraction, advanced search, modern UI/UX.
 30: - OAuth Authentication System: Google/GitHub OAuth, JWT management, production-grade error handling, 5/5 integration tests passing.
 31: - **Senior-Level Improvements**: Production-grade security, performance monitoring, comprehensive error handling, health checks, and frontend retry logic with Sonner integration.
 32: 
 33: ## 🚀 Production-Ready Improvements
 34: 
 35: ### Security & Performance Standards
 36: 
 37: - **Debug Logging Control**: Auth middleware conditionally logs only in development (`NODE_ENV !== "production"`)
 38: - **Type Safety**: Replace all `any` types with proper interfaces (use `AuthenticatedRequest` for authenticated routes)
 39: - **Rate Limiting**: Apply paper-specific rate limiters (`paperUploadLimiter`, `paperListLimiter`, `paperOperationLimiter`) to all paper endpoints
 40: - **Database Performance**: Add composite indexes for hot query paths (e.g., `Paper` queries by `uploaderId + workspaceId`)
 41: - **Performance Monitoring**: Use `performanceMonitor` middleware to track response times and add `X-Response-Time` headers
 42: 
 43: ### Error Handling Standards
 44: 
 45: - **Backend Error Classes**: Create feature-specific error classes (e.g., `PaperError`) extending `ApiError` for consistent error responses
 46: - **Standardized Error Format**: All errors follow `{ success: false, message: string, statusCode: number, errorCode?: string, details?: object }`
 47: - **Frontend Error Handling**: Use `useErrorHandler`, `useMutationErrorHandler`, and `useQueryErrorHandler` hooks with automatic retry logic
 48: - **Toast Integration**: Use Sonner via existing `ToastProvider` functions (`showErrorToast`, `showSuccessToast`) - never import `toast` directly
 49: 
 50: ### Health Check & Monitoring
 51: 
 52: - **Comprehensive Health Endpoints**:
 53:   - `/api/health` - Basic health status
 54:   - `/api/health/detailed` - Full system health (database, memory, environment)
 55:   - `/api/health/live` - Kubernetes liveness probe
 56:   - `/api/health/ready` - Kubernetes readiness probe
 57: - **Health Check Components**: Database connectivity, memory usage thresholds, required environment variables
 58: - **Performance Headers**: All requests include response time tracking for monitoring
 59: 
 60: ### Frontend Error Handling Patterns
 61: 
 62: - **Smart Retry Logic**: Auto-retry on 5xx/network errors, skip retry on 4xx client errors
 63: - **Error Classification**: Network errors, client errors (400-499), server errors (500+) with appropriate user messaging
 64: - **Toast Best Practices**: Use `showApiErrorToast()` from error handling utilities, respects network error retry logic
 65: - **RTK Query Integration**: Enhanced `apiSlice` with retry configuration and error transformation
 66: 
 67: ## Golden Rules (must-follow)
 68: 
 69: 1. TypeScript everywhere. No implicit `any`.
 70: 2. File naming: `module.type.ts` (e.g., `auth.controller.ts`, `user.service.ts`, `paper.validation.ts`, `paper.routes.ts`).
 71: 3. Validation with Zod on all inputs. Backend: use request validators + `validateRequest` middleware. Frontend: use `zodResolver` with React Hook Form.
 72: 4. Error handling: wrap route handlers with `catchAsync`; throw `ApiError` for expected failures; let `globalErrorHandler` map Prisma/validation errors.
 73: 5. Auth: Use standard Prisma upsert patterns for OAuth account linking and user/session records.
 74: 6. Database access (important):
 75: 
 76: - Prefer Prisma Client built-in model methods for standard CRUD, relations, and pagination.
 77: - Use `prisma.$queryRaw`/`prisma.$executeRaw` only for complex or performance-critical SQL that Prisma cannot express cleanly.
 78: - Never use `prisma.$queryRawUnsafe`.
 79: - Always paginate and filter; don’t ship unbounded queries.
 80: 
 81: 7. Security: Never log secrets. Enforce strict CORS, secure cookies, sanitize inputs, and validate all user-controlled data.
 82: 8. Performance: Paginate, add indexes, and cache hot reads when applicable (follow patterns already present in services).
 83: 9. Git hygiene: Conventional commits, small focused PRs, descriptive titles, linked issues, and stick to the current Roadmap phase.
 84: 
 85: ## Repository Structure (high-level)
 86: 
 87: - `apps/backend/src/app/`
 88:   - `modules/` feature folders (e.g., `Auth/`, `User/`, `Paper/`, `Collection/`)
 89:   - `middleware/` (`auth.ts`, `validateRequest.ts`, `globalErrorHandler.ts`)
 90:   - `shared/` (`prisma.ts`, `catchAsync.ts`, token utilities)
 91:   - `errors/` (`ApiError.ts`)
 92: - `apps/frontend/src/`
 93:   - `app/` (Next.js App Router pages and routes)
 94:   - `components/` + `components/customUI/form/` (e.g., `ScholarFlowForm.tsx`)
 95:   - `lib/validators.ts` (Zod schemas for common flows: auth, profile, paper, collection)
 96:   - `redux/`, `hooks/`, `lib/`, `middleware/`
 97: - `docs/` and `.github/` contain process and agent guidance
 98: 
 99: ## Day-to-day Commands
100: 
101: ```bash
102: # Bootstrap
103: yarn install
104: 
105: # Dev (both apps)
106: yarn dev:turbo
107: 
108: # Database (run from repo root)
109: yarn db:migrate    # Prisma migrate dev
110: yarn db:generate   # Generate Prisma client (+ TypedSQL artifacts)
111: yarn db:studio     # Open Prisma Studio
112: 
113: # Quality gates
114: yarn lint
115: yarn type-check
116: yarn build
117: yarn test
118: ```
119: 
120: ## Backend Patterns (what to copy)
121: 
122: - Use the shared Prisma instance from `apps/backend/src/app/shared/prisma.ts`.
123: - Prefer Prisma Client model methods for normal queries and mutations.
124: - Use raw SQL only when needed:
125:   - Use tagged template literals: ``await prisma.$queryRaw`SELECT \* FROM "User" WHERE id = ${userId}```
126:   - Or positional placeholders: `await prisma.$queryRaw('SELECT ... WHERE id = $1', userId)`
127:   - Never build SQL via string concatenation.
128: - Controllers: use `catchAsync` to wrap handlers and throw `ApiError` for invalid input, forbidden/unauthorized, and not-found cases.
129: - Validation: define Zod schemas and enforce with `validateRequest` middleware. Do not trust request bodies or query params.
130: - Error handling: `globalErrorHandler` maps `ApiError`, Prisma client errors, and generic failures to consistent responses.
131: - Auth middleware: `auth.ts` decodes JWT, loads user via Prisma/shared service access, and enforces roles/permissions.
132: 
133: ## Frontend Patterns (what to copy)
134: 
135: - App Router structure under `apps/frontend/src/app/*`.
136: - Forms: React Hook Form + Zod resolver; prefer shared schemas in `lib/validators.ts`.
137: - UI: ShadCN components; use `components/customUI/form/ScholarFlowForm.tsx` for common form wiring.
138: - State/data: RTK Query for API calls; align endpoints and shapes with backend controllers.
139: - Auth: NextAuth integration is present; keep environment vars in sync and avoid leaking secrets to the client.
140: 
141: ## Environment & Feature Flags
142: 
143: - Common variables used across the repo include (non-exhaustive): `DATABASE_URL`, `DIRECT_DATABASE_URL`, `NEXTAUTH_URL`, `NEXTAUTH_SECRET`, S3/AWS creds, `FRONTEND_URL`, `BACKEND_URL`, `USE_PGVECTOR`, and feature flags (e.g., upload toggles).
144: - When you introduce a new variable:
145:   - Add it to both apps' `.env.example`.
146:   - Reference it through a config module; never access `process.env` ad-hoc all over the code.
147:   - Document it in PR description and, if needed, in `docs/ENVIRONMENT.md`.
148: 
149: ## Phase 1 MVP Scope
150: 
151: 1. User Authentication & Profile Management ✅
152:    - Google OAuth + email/password, user profile, password reset.
153: 2. Paper Upload & Storage ✅
154:    - File processing, S3 storage, PDF metadata, advanced search, responsive UI.
155: 3. Basic Collections 🚧
156:    - Create/manage collections, assign papers, sharing/permissions, filtering.
157: 
158: ## Roadmap Discipline
159: 
160: - Always consult `Roadmap.md` and work sequentially: finish Phase 1 before starting Phase 2/3.
161: - Map any change to current phase. Defer out-of-phase ideas to “Next steps” in the PR.
162: - Keep PRs small and scoped to one milestone/feature.
163: 
164: ## Documentation Update Protocol
165: 
166: When changing authentication, UI/UX, or core patterns:
167: 
168: 1. Update `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` with new or revised patterns.
169: 2. Update `.github/instructions/*.md` with implementation guidance as needed.
170: 3. Update `docs/UI_DESIGN.md` for UI component or layout changes.
171: 4. Update `Roadmap.md` to reflect progress/completion.
172: 5. Add feature flags to both backend and frontend `.env.example` files.
173: 6. Update `README.md` only for major milestones or architecture changes.
174: 
175: ## Common Pitfalls to Avoid
176: 
177: - Using `npm` or adding `package-lock.json`. Use Yarn Berry only.
178: - Writing raw SQL with string concatenation or using `$queryRawUnsafe`.
179: - Overusing raw SQL when Prisma Client methods are sufficient.
180: - Skipping Zod validation or bypassing `validateRequest`.
181: - Throwing generic `Error` in controllers instead of `ApiError` or feature-specific error classes.
182: - Unbounded queries without pagination or missing indexes on hot paths.
183: - Adding environment variables without updating `.env.example` and docs.
184: - Mixing Phase 1 and later-phase features in a single PR.
185: - **Production Issues to Avoid**:
186:   - Logging debug info in production (check `NODE_ENV` first)
187:   - Using `any` types (use proper interfaces like `AuthenticatedRequest`)
188:   - Missing rate limiting on endpoints (especially upload/mutation endpoints)
189:   - Missing performance monitoring on critical paths
190:   - Importing `toast` directly (use `ToastProvider` functions instead)
191:   - Not handling BigInt serialization in JSON responses (cast to integer in SQL)
192:   - Missing error retry logic for network/server errors in frontend
