 38: # Scholar-Flow AI Agent Instructions
 39: 
 40: ## Product
 41: Scholar-Flow is an AI-powered research paper SaaS collaboration platform.
 42: Target users: researchers, students, professors, academic teams.
 43: Version: 1.1.0 (20 releases, 803 commits)
 44: 
 45: Core features:
 46: - Research paper upload with AI metadata extraction (title/author/abstract)
 47: - TipTap rich text editor: auto-save, drafts, export PDF/DOCX
 48: - Collections and workspaces with role-based access
 49: - Email sharing with permission management (view/edit)
 50: - Stripe billing: checkout, webhooks, customer portal
 51: - Admin dashboard: real-time CPU/memory/storage/DB metrics (10s refresh)
 52: - pgvector semantic search
 53: - PDF preview (secure iframe)
 54: - AWS S3 for all file storage
 55: 
 56: ---
 57: 
 58: ## Monorepo Structure
 59: Root: Turborepo v2.5.8 + Yarn Berry v4.9.2
 60:   apps/frontend/    → Next.js 15 app (package: @scholar-flow/frontend)
 61:   apps/backend/     → Express.js API (package: @scholar-flow/backend)
 62:   docs/             → Documentation
 63:   .cursor/rules/    → Cursor AI guidelines (read-only)
 64:   figma-make/       → Figma-generated component references
 65: 
 66: ---
 67: 
 68: ## Frontend (apps/frontend)
 69: Framework: Next.js 15 App Router
 70: Language: TypeScript strict mode
 71: State: Redux Toolkit Query (RTK Query) — NOT plain Redux
 72: Auth: NextAuth.js (Google OAuth, GitHub OAuth, email/password)
 73: UI: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
 74: Forms: React Hook Form + Zod
 75: Editor: TipTap (tiptap-extension-resizable-image, tiptap-resizable-image)
 76: Virtualization: react-window
 77: Sanitization: sanitize-html
 78: File structure:
 79:   apps/frontend/src/app/         → App Router pages and layouts
 80:   apps/frontend/components/      → Reusable UI components
 81:   apps/frontend/lib/             → Utilities, auth config, API clients
 82:   apps/frontend/redux/           → RTK Query slices and store config
 83: 
 84: ---
 85: 
 86: ## Backend (apps/backend)
 87: Runtime: Node.js >=22, Express.js, TypeScript
 88: ORM: Prisma 6.16.3 (always use --sql flag: yarn db:generate)
 89: Database: PostgreSQL + pgvector extension
 90: Auth: JWT + bcrypt (refresh tokens, rate limiting)
 91: Storage: AWS S3 (presigned URLs only — never expose credentials)
 92: Payments: Stripe (checkout + webhooks + customer portal)
 93: Validation: Zod schemas
 94: File structure:
 95:   apps/backend/src/app/          → Controllers, routes, middleware
 96:   apps/backend/prisma/           → schema.prisma + migrations
 97:   apps/backend/scripts/          → Seed and utility scripts
 98: 
 99: ---
100: 
101: ## Exact Commands (always use Yarn — never npm, never pnpm)
102: yarn dev:turbo           → start both apps via Turborepo
103: yarn dev:frontend        → frontend only
104: yarn dev:backend         → backend only
105: yarn build               → production build (both)
106: yarn lint                → ESLint (both)
107: yarn type-check          → TypeScript check (both, needs build first)
108: yarn test                → test suite
109: yarn format              → Prettier
110: yarn clean               → clean all build artifacts
111: yarn db:migrate          → prisma migrate dev
112: yarn db:generate         → prisma generate --sql  ← always --sql
113: yarn db:studio           → Prisma Studio
114: yarn db:seed             → seed database
115: yarn db:reset            → prisma migrate reset --force
116: yarn setup               → install + generate (use for fresh setup)
117: 
118: ---
119: 
120: ## Architecture Rules
121: Frontend and backend are fully decoupled — REST API only.
122: Frontend: UI, RTK Query, NextAuth sessions, Redux state.
123: Backend: business logic, Prisma, S3, Stripe, JWT.
124: NEVER import backend code into frontend.
125: NEVER use Prisma in frontend.
126: NEVER bypass the REST API layer.
127: NEVER store files locally in production — use S3.
128: NEVER expose AWS credentials or S3 bucket names in frontend code.
129: 
130: ---
131: 
132: ## Authentication Rules
133: Frontend: NextAuth.js only — Google/GitHub OAuth + email/password.
134: Backend: JWT + bcrypt + refresh tokens.
135: NEVER suggest better-auth or any auth replacement.
136: NEVER bypass auth middleware on protected routes.
137: NEVER store raw passwords — always bcrypt.
138: Session validation: backend verifies JWT, frontend uses NextAuth session.
139: 
140: ---
141: 
142: ## Stripe Rules (critical — production money flows)
143: Webhooks are the ONLY source of truth for subscription status.
144: NEVER update subscription roles from frontend.
145: ALWAYS verify Stripe webhook signatures before processing.
146: Customer portal accessed via backend endpoint only.
147: Webhook handlers MUST be idempotent (safe to replay).
148: Test mode and live mode keys are different — never mix them.
149: Before touching billing code: read the webhook handler first.
150: 
151: ---
152: 
153: ## AWS S3 Rules (critical — user data)
154: ALL file operations use presigned URLs.
155: NEVER stream files through the backend if avoidable.
156: NEVER hardcode S3 bucket names in frontend.
157: NEVER expose AWS_ACCESS_KEY or AWS_SECRET in frontend.
158: Image resizing happens server-side before S3 upload.
159: PDF upload goes through backend S3 route — not direct from browser.
160: 
161: ---
162: 
163: ## pgvector Rules (critical — AI features)
164: pgvector powers semantic search on research papers.
165: NEVER drop vector columns or vector indexes.
166: NEVER run prisma migrate reset in production.
167: Always use yarn db:generate --sql after schema changes.
168: Embedding dimension is fixed — never change vector size in production.
169: queryRaw is required for pgvector operations (Prisma limitation).
170: 
171: ---
172: 
173: ## TipTap Editor Rules
174: Auto-save uses debounce — never call save on every keystroke.
175: Draft and publish states are separate fields.
176: Image uploads go to S3 via backend — never base64 inline.
177: Export PDF/DOCX uses server-side rendering — do not use browser print.
178: resizable-image extension is installed — use it for image nodes.
179: 
180: ---
181: 
182: ## RTK Query Rules
183: All API calls go through RTK Query slices in apps/frontend/redux/.
184: Use cache invalidation tags correctly — providesTags / invalidatesTags.
185: NEVER make raw fetch calls in components.
186: NEVER put API logic inside React components.
187: Optimistic updates must have rollback on error.
188: 
189: ---
190: 
191: ## Admin Dashboard Rules
192: Metrics refresh every 10 seconds — do not change this interval.
193: CPU calculation uses os.cpus() idle/total delta — do not simplify.
194: Storage estimation: 10x actual DB usage, minimum 100GB baseline.
195: Health thresholds: green <50%, blue <70%, yellow <85%, red >85%.
